# Coding Rules

## Philosophy

Write production-quality code that is simple, readable, maintainable,
and consistent with the existing codebase.

## General Rules

-   Follow the project's established architecture.
-   Prefer patterns already present in the project when they correctly
    use the installed stack. Do not copy scattered framework misuse.
-   Follow Framework-Native First in `00-platform`: establish whether the
    installed stack already provides the capability before custom code,
    abstractions, or new dependencies.
-   For versions, libraries, frameworks, APIs, and implementation patterns,
    follow Current-First Engineering in `00-platform` — do not default to
    historically common patterns from model memory.
-   Keep implementations focused on the requested task.
-   Do not introduce unnecessary abstractions.
-   Do not perform unrelated refactoring.
-   Do not opportunistically expand the current task. Capture unrelated
    bugs, refactors, and features as work items (`70-work-items`).
-   Optimize for readability over cleverness.

## Stack-native architecture

Detect the stack before applying a framework profile. Follow
`21-stack-architecture.mdc`.

Template views render prepared data. Conditions, loops, formatting, and
class selection may stay in the template. Queries, persistence, substantial
transformation, content parsing, and service orchestration belong in the
framework's own preparation layer. Do not impose Laravel layer names on
other frameworks, and do not apply MVC view restrictions to React or
similar components.

## Tailwind

A specialization of Framework-Native First in `00-platform`.

When the project uses Tailwind, treat it as a compositional system.
Use native utilities and theme primitives. Do not reconstruct first-class
Tailwind behavior by hand, and do not invent a parallel CSS abstraction
layer on top of Tailwind.

This standard takes precedence over vendor-skill Tailwind/CSS shortcuts
(Instruction Priority in `00-platform`). Hallmark may provide design
reasoning, token recipes, or CSS examples. Translate those into this
order of preference; do not copy them as one-off arbitrary utilities
merely to match a recipe or comp.

Current-First Engineering in `00-platform` chooses the mechanism: inspect
the installed Tailwind version, then use that version's current official
configuration. On v4, that is CSS-first architecture (`@theme`, and other
current v4 APIs). On v3, that is the v3 config/theme conventions. Do not
upgrade Tailwind merely to satisfy this standard.

Use Tailwind's standard utility scale when an equivalent exists.
Arbitrary values are reserved for genuine design requirements that
cannot reasonably be expressed with the existing standard utilities or
project tokens.

This applies especially to typography, spacing, sizing, gaps,
padding/margins, and widths/heights where standard utilities are
appropriate.

Preferred decision path:

1. Existing project token or existing utility
2. Standard Tailwind utility
3. Project-level Tailwind theme token (`@theme` or version-appropriate
   configuration) when the value is repeated or represents a
   design-system decision
4. Arbitrary value only for a genuine one-off exception

Do not use an arbitrary value merely because it matches a design comp
more precisely. Reasonable visual approximation using the standard
Tailwind scale is preferred unless exact sizing is materially necessary.

Undesirable default behavior:

- `text-[1.2rem]` when `text-xl` is appropriate
- `text-[2.35rem]` chosen simply to approximate a comp
- `mt-[17px]` when the standard spacing scale is reasonably equivalent
- repeated arbitrary values that should instead become a project token

Arbitrary values remain valid when genuinely necessary. There is no
absolute ban. When an arbitrary value remains in newly written or
materially modified code, explain why the standard scale or project
tokens were insufficient. Use `guardrail-allow-arbitrary` on that line
or the previous line (see `docs/contracts/features/copy-and-tailwind-guardrails.md`).
Do not create extra documentation for every legitimate exception.

- Start with the most appropriate native utility or primitive: `container`,
  spacing scale, colors, typography, font families, radii, shadows,
  transitions, breakpoints, and other first-class Tailwind APIs.
- Compose utilities for local differences. Override only the property that
  actually differs (`container max-w-5xl`).
- Configure repeated site-wide design decisions once, using that Tailwind
  version's theme/configuration — not by scattering reconstructed class
  strings through templates.
- Do not add custom CSS or a semantic class merely because several
  utilities appear together. Prefer `container max-w-5xl` over a one-off
  `.narrow-wrap` class for that combination.
- Do not add a semantic class, custom declaration, or `@apply` wrapper
  to relocate styling that utilities already express.
- CSS may contain framework imports, theme definitions, font faces, and
  CSS that cannot reasonably be done with utilities. That includes
  vendor or integration selectors that utilities cannot target.
- A legitimate exception names the concrete limitation on the finding
  or the previous line: `guardrail-allow-architecture: ...`
  Do not use an arbitrary utility instead when a standard utility or
  token works.
- Avoid custom CSS when native composition already solves the problem.
- This Tailwind section applies only when Tailwind is the styling system
  for the file being changed. Plain CSS, Bootstrap, CSS Modules, and
  CSS-in-JS keep their own conventions. A Tailwind dependency elsewhere
  in the repository does not make those files Tailwind files. Scope and
  the mechanical check: `docs/contracts/features/stack-native-architecture.md`.

Default site content wrapper: prefer `container`, with the site's normal
container behavior configured centrally, over reconstructing
`mx-auto w-full max-w-[…] px-… lg:px-…` at every call site.

Apply this to new and touched markup. Do not mass-refactor existing
reconstructed utilities unless the task is to adopt the standard.

When the project uses WordPress with Tailwind and `theme.json`, presets
and editorial element styles follow `.cursor/rules/36-wordpress-theme.mdc`.
That rule does not apply to other stacks.

## Safety

Never:

-   Commit code unless explicitly instructed.
-   Modify dependencies without approval.
-   Perform destructive database operations without approval.
-   Deploy to any environment unless explicitly instructed.

## Public submissions

Contact forms, lead forms, quote requests, registration, comments, and
any other public endpoint that creates a record, sends mail, or
triggers costly work follow
`docs/contracts/features/spam-and-abuse-protection.md`.
Load that contract when building or changing one. Record the result in
the project's `docs/contracts/features/public-submissions.md`
(`not applicable` when the project has no such surface).

## Quality

Before considering a task complete:

-   Verify the implementation with proportional, lightweight checks
    appropriate to the task (lint, types, targeted unit/feature tests,
    build when genuinely necessary, framework-native validation). Do not
    escalate visual or frontend changes into Playwright or browser
    automation unless `60-browser-qa` authorizes it (explicit request, or
    the bounded significant-UI Mode B visual pass in `40-ui`).
-   For work that writes or modifies public-facing copy, templates,
    components, or styling, run `scripts/check-guardrails.sh` before
    reporting DONE. Lint, tests, and build passing do not imply that
    check ran, and a passing check does not prove architecture
    compliance. A failed check is not a green completion: fix the
    violation, or use the documented exception marker and explain the
    concrete limitation. When views, components, data preparation, or
    styling change, include the short architecture review from
    `docs/contracts/features/stack-native-architecture.md`.
-   Load relevant documentation contracts via `docs/README.md` (not the full
    documentation tree).
-   Resolve the documentation outcome: update affected contracts, explicitly
    state no updates are required, or report a documentation conflict.
-   Ensure changes remain consistent with the project's architecture.
